WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:04.910
Robert: 7 million American men are outside the
labor force.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.599
It's a problem we have to address.

00:00:06.599 --> 00:00:11.099
So, it used to be that virtually all men between
25 and 55 worked.

00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:16.930
But now we're down to about 88%, which means
7 million American men are outside of the

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:17.930
labor force.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:22.560
Which made me think, what if we got those
men back into work.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.789
How would we do it?

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:29.920
Harry: So, the question is, what causes so many
men, especially less educated men to drop

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.570
out of the workforce?

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:37.870
Probably the biggest factor is that a generation
or two ago, these men faced good paying manufacturing

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:42.640
jobs, but most of those jobs have disappeared,
and if they remain, they pay much less than

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.700
they used to.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.940
They are either gonna find low-wage jobs available,
but very unattractive, or the jobs themselves

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.470
won't be available because they're living
in depressed communities where so much of

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.050
the employment has disappeared.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:00.570
A lot of these men are now on some kind of
public benefit program.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.251
Now, these programs do a lot of good, but
the downside of these programs is that they

00:01:04.251 --> 00:01:06.130
can discourage work effort.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:10.079
In addition, a large fraction of them have
opioid dependencies.

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:13.979
A lot of them will not be able to pass a drug
test to be able to take a job.

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:19.810
There is a quite large population of men with
criminal records, employers are very reluctant

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.229
to hire these men.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:25.619
And on top of that, a lot of these men have
children of whom they don't have custody.

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:29.869
A lot of these men fall behind in their child
support payments.

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:33.929
Once they are in arrears, the tax rates on
their earnings are very high and they have

00:01:33.929 --> 00:01:37.220
strong incentives to disappear from the formal
labor market.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.159
They might work off books for cash.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:46.880
Brent: It is critical from the standpoint of
construction, manufacturing, other heavy industries,

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.329
that we American workers who are sitting on
the sidelines back into gainful employment.

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:58.689
We need hundreds of thousands of new construction
workers to address a housing shortage.

00:01:58.689 --> 00:02:00.820
Where are we going to get those workers?

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.249
If you talk to those who know the construction
sector, they will tell you we are already

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:08.709
using every available worker.

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:12.730
So it's one thing to say to men, "You need
to go back to work."

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.990
But then you have to actually create jobs
for them to go to.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:21.820
Harry: Men, who have only a high school diploma
or even less than that, simply don't have

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.320
the skills that employers value.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.880
We wanna be improving the skills that these
men bring to the labor market, and there's

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.630
a few different ways of doing that.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.150
One answer is what we call sector based training.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:39.350
Industry representatives come together with
community colleges to create training programs

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:44.170
and curricula, to train workers for those
jobs that are in fact, available.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:48.620
The other approach that we have a lot of confidence
in is apprenticeship.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.440
In these cases, they're being paid while they're
training, and they can see the direct relevance

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.180
of the training to the work.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:02.320
And employers like it too because you're training
the worker exactly for the skills that the

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.030
employer needs.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:10.010
For those who can't find jobs, we favor some
expansion of tax credits paid to the workers

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.230
to make those jobs more attractive.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.430
The best example we have of that is something
called the Earned Income Tax Credit.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:21.680
Robert: One other way to increase labor force
participation among men is to help those who

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:28.600
are non-custodial parents, both work and pay
child support, by supplementing their wages

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.840
with an Earned Income Tax Credit.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.420
It rewards them for working and supporting
their families.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.290
Raising men's skills is not the only issue
we face.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.390
We also have to address public assistance
programs that encourage men to stay out of

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.320
the labor force and not work.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:52.901
And the way we do that is, we make those programs
say to their applicants for them, "How can

00:03:52.901 --> 00:03:54.950
we help you get a job?"

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.340
One of those programs is the food stamp program,
which does a lot in America, reduce hunger

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.680
and lower poverty.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:05.640
But it also has an increasing number of adults
who are receiving food stamps and are reporting

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.150
no earnings.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.970
And they're not disabled, and they're not
elderly, and they're not children.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.360
They're able-bodied adults who could be in
the workforce and aren't.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.979
In order to make these assistance programs
more effective at moving people into work,

00:04:18.979 --> 00:04:23.990
there has to be an understanding between the
agency and the recipient, that the recipient

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.750
needs to do something to advance their economic
situation.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.530
They need to move toward work.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.159
And if they don't, there have to be consequences.

00:04:32.159 --> 00:04:37.340
Brent: One project that's going on right now
in Baltimore, Maryland is the redevelopment

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.150
of Port Covington neighborhood.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:45.169
The city of Baltimore is putting in $535 million
worth of tax subsidies.

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:48.039
A development group is matching those funds.

00:04:48.039 --> 00:04:53.150
But in order to get the tax subsidies, the
developers have committed that 30% of the

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:57.490
new jobs created will go to residents of the
City of Baltimore.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:01.930
Some of those jobs need to be reserved for
people who are currently out of the workforce.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:08.199
The reality of our labor market is that, over
the last 40 or 50 years, we've seen a dramatic

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:13.229
shift, where jobs involving heavy industry
work have been off-shored.

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:17.629
We've experienced deindustrialization, and
the president made a commitment during his

00:05:17.629 --> 00:05:20.430
campaign to bring some of those jobs back.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.979
Members of the House, and Senate, and the
president ought to consider the creation of

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:27.169
a requirement for community benefit agreements.

00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:32.039
That entities receiving federal tax subsidies
would have to set aside a certain number of

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:37.249
jobs to be created for people who have exhausted
their unemployment benefits and present to

00:05:37.249 --> 00:05:42.479
the government their plan for bringing these
people back into the labor force.

00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:47.910
Robert: It's not just the economy that will be
strengthened by getting men back into work,

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.990
it's the men themselves because when they're
working, they're much less likely to be poor,

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:57.290
they're much less likely to be unhealthy,
and they're much more likely to be strong

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.539
providers for their families and their children.

00:06:01.539 --> 00:06:08.539
A good way to illustrate the inadequacy of
our public assistance programs is when I went

00:06:08.539 --> 00:06:11.169
out and talked to people who are receiving
benefits.

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:16.590
I talked to a man who said, "That program
is very good at getting me an EBT card, but

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.330
it does nothing to get me a job."

